% A device method closes what it opens.
%
% A device method runs with a dictionary current: the device's own for
% the methods the machinery calls, a scratch one of its own for the
% helpers underneath them. The method opens it on the way in and takes it
% off on the way out, and every name the body reads or defines is read
% and defined in there. An error part way through the body is a way out
% of it like returning is, so the close is owed on that path too -- and a
% method that skips it leaves its dictionary current for whatever runs
% next. The error handler runs in it, the program that catches the error
% carries on in it, and a definition either of them makes lands in a
% dictionary that is not theirs. Where the dictionary is the class's own
% scratch, which lives in global memory, the program's next definition is
% not merely misplaced but refused.
%
% PLRM 3.11 says what an operator leaves behind when it fails; the
% dictionary stack is the simple half of it, and the rule needs no
% adjudicating: a dictionary this method pushed is this method's to pop,
% whichever way it left.
%
% So every method of every class is driven to a failure here and the
% dictionary stack is measured across it -- the raster classes, which
% paint pixels into a page they hold, and the vector classes, which write
% the marks out in a page description instead. The measurement is the
% whole test -- the errors are provoked deliberately and nothing is
% asserted about which one arrives, only that the stack the method was
% handed is the stack it gives back.
%
% Run through `xpost -q --no-sandbox -d null` by run-ps-test.sh: prints
% SUCCESS iff every method of every class gave the dictionary stack back.
%
% Two ways this test could pass while checking nothing, and what holds
% each of them:
%
%   A method renamed or dropped stops being driven, and a roster naming
%   it goes on passing because nothing answers to the name. Every method
%   named below has to be found on some device, or the run fails.
%
%   A trial that provokes no error measures a method that returned
%   normally, which every method does. Every method named below has to
%   have raised on some class and some operand shape, except the ones
%   named as having no failure path at all -- and those have to still
%   have none.

/failcount 0 def
/assert { exch { pop }{ (FAIL: ) print print (\n) print
    /failcount failcount 1 add store } ifelse } def

% the depth the dictionary stack stands at outside any method
/d0 countdictstack def

% The operand shapes a method is driven to a failure with. A method takes
% its operands from the stack, and the two shapes are the two ways that
% can go wrong: more operands than any method here takes, none of them a
% type any body can use, and one such operand, which leaves the body
% reaching past what it was given. Neither is a shape a caller should
% produce; what they are for is to reach the failure paths a caller
% cannot be relied on to reach.
%
% The operands are null rather than a name or a string because a method
% may store what it is handed in a dictionary of its own, and one of
% those dictionaries belongs to the class, which lives in global memory:
% a simple object stores there, where a composite object of this
% program's own is refused before the body has got anywhere.
/shapes [ { null null null null null null } { null } ] def
/shapenames [ (six unusable operands) (one unusable operand) ] def

% The methods held, each with whether it takes the device dictionary as
% its last operand. The ones that do are the suite the device machinery
% calls (data/image.ps); the ones that do not are the helpers a method
% calls with the device already current, which are reached here from
% outside that scope, as any caller that has only the device can reach
% them. Both kinds open a dictionary and so both kinds owe the close.
%
% The vector classes add the methods a device that keeps its marks
% rather than its pixels is asked for -- a whole path to fill or stroke,
% a clip shape it could not reduce to a rectangle, a sampled image -- and
% the writers that put a page or a document out. The recording class adds
% the page it paints at Emit by playing the marks it wrote down into a
% device that does paint.
/roster [
    /Create      true
    /Destroy     true
    /PutPix      true
    /GetPix      true
    /DrawLine    true
    /DrawRect    true
    /FillRect    true
    /FillPoly    true
    /FillPath    true
    /StrokePath  true
    /ClipPath    true
    /PaintImage  true
    /Ground      true
    /Emit        true
    /.playpage   true
    /.moveband   true
    /.registersep true
    /.emitpage   true
    /.begindoc   true
    /.endfile    true
    /.intersect  false
    /.writehead  false
    /.writetail  false
    /.writepage  false
] def

% One body of this shape is not here, and is named rather than left to be
% assumed covered: the class-to-instance copy every class stores under
% /.copydict opens the copy to write the dimensions into it. It is
% reached from here -- it is a method of every device below -- but not
% driven to the failure inside that open, which wants a class carrying a
% width and no height, and the operand shapes above carry nulls and the
% device and can build no such thing. What holds that body is the
% dictionary stack read across it directly, and reaching it here wants a
% third operand shape built out of a class rather than out of nulls.
%
% The methods with no failure path of their own, named rather than
% counted: each is a body that cannot raise whatever it is handed, so no
% trial provokes anything and none is expected to. A method that acquires
% a failure path is caught by the far side of the same check.
/undrivable [ /Destroy ] def

% The classes held: every class the interpreter can make. Each is held
% twice -- as the class's own suite, and as an instance finished the way
% the device machinery finishes one, which replaces some of the suite
% with the compiled rasterisers. A page is marked through the second, so
% the second is the one a program's error arrives in.
%
% A class is reached through its maker, and every maker lives in the
% private dictionary: most are procedures a boot file left there, and
% the recording class's is an operator its driver installs beside them. That class holds its page as marks and paints it by
% playing them into a device that does paint, so its marking methods,
% Create, GetPix and Destroy are compiled operators that open no
% dictionary; what it has of the shape measured here is /Ground and the
% page it plays at Emit, which are written in PostScript and owe the same
% close as any other body.
/makers [
    /newPGMIMAGEdevice (pgm)
    /newPPMIMAGEdevice (ppm)
    /newPBMIMAGEdevice (pbm)
    /newTIFFIMAGEdevice (tiff)
    /newbboxdevice (bbox)
    /newPDFWRITEdevice (pdfwrite)
    /newSVGWRITEdevice (svgwrite)
    /newDSCWRITEdevice (dscwrite)
    /newrecorddevice (record)
] def

% The drivers a run brings in on demand. A class a boot file defined is in
% the private dictionary from the start; one a C driver defines is not
% there, and neither is its maker, until the driver has been loaded. Each
% loader named here is run before the walk, so that every maker above is
% there to be found by the time one is looked for.
/loaders [ /loadrecorddevice ] def
loaders {
    dup where { exch get exec }{
        (FAIL: ) print 64 string cvs print
        ( loads a driver this interpreter does not have; a class below cannot be made\n) print
        /failcount failcount 1 add store
    } ifelse
} forall

% /maker  findmaker  proc|null
% The maker, wherever it lives. The private dictionary is looked in by
% name because it is not on the dictionary stack; everything else is
% found the way a program would find it.
/findmaker {
    dup .privatedict exch known
        { .privatedict exch get }
        { dup where { exch get }{ pop null } ifelse }
    ifelse
} def

% A name this interpreter cannot open for writing, so that the emission
% methods are driven to a failure rather than made to write a page. It
% reaches every class whose Emit opens a file; the bounding-box class
% writes its report to the standard output instead, so its Emit runs to
% the end and the empty boxes it reports appear in this test's output.
% They are that device working, not this test failing.
/unopenable (/nonexistent-xpost-dictstack-guard/page.out) def

% /maker finished  make  device
% One device to run one trial against, made fresh: a trial provokes an
% error part way through a method, which leaves the instance in whatever
% state the method had reached, and the next trial is owed a device that
% has not been half written to.
/make {
    /mk.finish exch def
    dup findmaker
    dup null eq {
        pop
        (FAIL: ) print 64 string cvs print
        ( is a maker no dictionary answers to; the makers have moved\n) print
        /failcount failcount 1 add store
        null
    }{
        exch pop
        20 10 3 -1 roll exec
        dup /.state get /.outputfile unopenable put
        mk.finish { dup 1 1 .privatedict /.completedevice get exec } if
    } ifelse
} def

% what was seen of each method across every trial
/present 32 dict def
/raised 32 dict def

% /maker finished (tag) /method shape  drive  -
% Run one method of one device with one operand shape, and measure the
% dictionary stack across it.
/drive {
    /dv.shape exch def
    /dv.method exch def
    /dv.tag exch def
    /dv.finish exch def
    /dv.maker exch def
    dv.maker dv.finish make
    dup null eq { pop }{
        /dv.dev exch def
        dv.dev dv.method known {
            present dv.method true put
            count /dv.count exch def
            { shapes dv.shape get exec
              takesdev dv.method get { dv.dev } if
              dv.dev dv.method get exec } stopped
            % What the trial found is read and the dictionary stack put
            % back before any of it is written down. A dictionary left
            % current is what this measures, and a definition made while
            % one is lands in it -- which is the whole complaint, and
            % would be recorded where the next line cannot read it.
            countdictstack d0 sub
            dup 0 gt { dup { end } repeat } if
            /dv.delta exch def
            /dv.raised exch def
            dv.raised { raised dv.method true put } if
            % the operands a failed method left behind are not this
            % test's subject: PLRM 3.11 settles those separately, and a
            % trial that read its own report off the stack would report
            % the leavings instead
            count dv.count sub dup 0 gt { { pop } repeat }{ pop } ifelse
            dv.delta 0 ne {
                (FAIL: ) print dv.tag print
                dv.finish { ( finished) }{ ( class) } ifelse print ( ) print
                dv.method 64 string cvs print
                ( left ) print dv.delta 20 string cvs print
                ( dictionaries on the stack, given ) print
                shapenames dv.shape get print (\n) print
                /failcount failcount 1 add store
            } if
        } if
    } ifelse
} def

% the roster read as a dictionary, for the operand question above, and as
% a list of names, for the walk below
/methodnames [ 0 2 roster length 2 sub { roster exch get } for ] def
/takesdev roster length dict def
0 2 roster length 2 sub {
    /ri exch def
    takesdev roster ri get roster ri 1 add get put
} for

% every class, finished and unfinished, every method, every shape
0 2 makers length 2 sub {
    /mi exch def
    [ false true ] {
        /fin exch def
        methodnames {
            /m exch def
            0 1 shapes length 1 sub {
                makers mi get fin makers mi 1 add get m
                5 -1 roll drive
            } for
        } forall
    } forall
} for

% --- the two ways this test could go quiet -------------------------
% Every method named in the roster is a method some device has. One that
% is not has been renamed or dropped, and the roster has been driving
% nothing under that name.
methodnames {
    /m exch def
    present m known not {
        (FAIL: ) print m 64 string cvs print
        ( is in the roster and on no class; it has been renamed or dropped\n) print
        /failcount failcount 1 add store
    } if
} forall

% Every method the roster drives raised somewhere, except the ones named
% as having no failure path -- and those raised nowhere. A method that
% stops raising is a method this test measures nothing across; one that
% starts is a method the exemption no longer describes.
methodnames {
    /m exch def
    /want true def
    undrivable { m eq { /want false def } if } forall
    raised m known want ne {
        (FAIL: ) print m 64 string cvs print
        want {
            ( was driven to no failure by any trial; nothing was measured across it\n) print
        }{
            ( is named as having no failure path and raised anyway\n) print
        } ifelse
        /failcount failcount 1 add store
    } if
} forall

% The trials themselves ran: a roster that emptied, a maker list that
% emptied or a shape list that emptied would leave every check above with
% nothing to say and this test reporting success.
methodnames length 0 gt (the method roster is not empty) assert
makers length 0 gt (the class roster is not empty) assert
shapes length 0 gt (the shape roster is not empty) assert
present length 0 gt (some method of some class was found and driven) assert

failcount 0 eq { (SUCCESS) = } { (FAILURES: ) print failcount = } ifelse
